{"title":"Mock Geometry AIME 2011 Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_Geometry_AIME_2011_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> are chosen at random on a circle. The probability that there exists a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> inside an equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/1/9b1815ca31a0a4b18b725b690862a84de67aed84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1B_1C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473ad025c4a94bd2d1c35abc5fa174f83696c671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA_1=BC,PB_1=AC,PC_1=AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"276\" height=\"16\" /> can be expressed in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/f/bff0adf1df47377b4e30273a51b9d02e57b2f307.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m} {n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"24\" height=\"33\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46ba6ed097cc88ded349bf99eea89817f135f470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"11\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db26f75f6218a382840f0ed34f5d36bcff61d33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_Geometry_AIME_2011_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> be the three distances from point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to each of the vertices, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> being the longest distance. Let's consider the case in which point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is actually on the line:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/9/0b90503b9eee212cbf52c4069d85a8ce614c8c36.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.75cm); draw((0,1+4*sqrt(3))--(8,1+4*sqrt(3))); draw((0,1+4*sqrt(3))--(4,1)); draw((8,1+4*sqrt(3))--(4,1)); draw((6,1+4*sqrt(3))--(4,1)); label(&quot;$P$&quot;,(6,1+4*sqrt(3)),NNW); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(5,1+2*sqrt(3)),NNW); label(&quot;$y$&quot;, (3.5,1+4*sqrt(3)),NW); label(&quot;$z$&quot;, (7.5,1+4*sqrt(3)),NW); [/asy]\" width=\"285\" height=\"268\" />\n</p><p>In this case, we can use Stewart's Theorem to find the relationship between the three variables.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/6/93699772154cee7af4c869bd2c3c7d1057b0ae48.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[yz(y+z)+x^2(y+z)=y(y+z)^2+z(y+z)^2\\]\" width=\"358\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/9/039b1a4570b4afefecc86c433190b4af3bc4fb92.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[yz+x^2=y(y+z)+z(y+z)\\]\" width=\"232\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/1/c519301e7a6450e8fe0c85cc1b68a6af1443974e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[yz+x^2=y^2+z^2+2yz\\]\" width=\"188\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/7/727799ef32f9ceb2b3ab83eedf896432476a68eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2=y^2+z^2+yz\\]\" width=\"137\" height=\"18\" />\nThis can be manipulated into the Law of Cosines, with an angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12c81f307533c5f5defc7c713ba1388d17ac2ac0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2=y^2+z^2-2yz(\\cos120^{\\circ})\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>In order for point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to be inside the equilateral triangle:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/5/8150c9c22ad57d06635c0d18dc32d2a3916dd77f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2&lt;y^2+z^2-2yz(\\cos120^{\\circ})\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"19\" />\nThus, Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> (which has sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/2/692e3bee67c91837acbdcf63862b3b3c94366f28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />) cannot have any angles greater than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. Next, we can fix point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f0cd95f95238159d02dbcea0aca632c2928991c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" /> mark of a unit circle, as shown below:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/2/77219cf84ab3037e58c326f186c927932559ef8c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5cm); draw((-4,0)--(4,0),Arrows); draw((0,-4)--(0,4),Arrows); draw(circle((0,0),3),red+linewidth(1)); dot((-3,0)); label(&quot;$A$&quot;, (-3,0), NW); [/asy]\" width=\"192\" height=\"192\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the places points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> should be on the circle, such that Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> has all angles less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> is OUTSIDE of the red outline, but inside of the green outline shown in the graph below:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/e/c5eee347c56a5f173c496d29d1163c8371b5f22a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.7cm); draw((-1,0)--(7,0),Arrows); draw((0,-1)--(0,7),Arrows); draw((0,6)--(6,6)--(6,0)--(0,0)--cycle,green+linewidth(1)); draw((1,1)--(3,1)--(5,3)--(5,5)--(3,5)--(1,3)--cycle, red); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(7,0),SE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(0,7),NW); [/asy]\" width=\"288\" height=\"288\" />\n</p><p>The red hexagon has vertices at coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24dfc15e4cea625912b4ec71dec1c9e362557da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(60,60),(180,60),(300,180),(300,300),(180,300),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"392\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/2/662a2f2185bda9232e9234cf546e8640f72397fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(60,180)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />, while the green square has vertices at coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/1/6813abd5c8e259143078b135947357a8ac867565.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0),(0,360),(360,360),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"195\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/4/a143e375345ba1c8a0afd462301ffcb6454ef8dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(360,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />. Each other these coordinates represent positions for points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/5/f45db504afa53dd0002bbeaf6201e3b17a5686a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(240, 181)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> means point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/d/84d832426c089fa4c86b211bcfe562bca40750ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(240^{\\circ})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"51\" height=\"20\" /> on the unit circle, while point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/b/dbb0c382ffda7a364c6efc801e8e23f472f9dca8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(181^{\\circ})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" /> on the unit circle.\n</p><p>Therefore, the probability of points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccf6d387ce491c0ca100c134021f21f616d75162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> forming a triangle with angles less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/7/f973a594f8fb7b4407a7f7d10d58c1e78c32e286.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1-\\frac{2\\cdot120^2+2\\cdot\\frac{120^2}{2}}{360^2}\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"44\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/2/8b2f8de836b1ecb29a8d33d2cbe5bf4e3c618430.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=1-\\frac{1}{3}\\]\" width=\"61\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/1/281fad44fa54fec74226e8be83ad6ceae3bab543.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=\\frac{2}{3}\\]\" width=\"29\" height=\"37\" />.\nAnd the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bff5e08c908f21d2a46f5b07fab2c254cbf0b23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+3=\\boxed{005}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"23\" />\n- Solution by adyj\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Punxsutawney_Phil\">Video Solution by Punxsutawney Phil</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_Geometry_AIME_2011_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Punxsutawney Phil\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtube.com/watch?v=q0lUoXmkAyk&amp;t=794s\">https://youtube.com/watch?v=q0lUoXmkAyk&amp;t=794s</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018041003\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 268/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9896/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:13871:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018041003 and revision id 184773. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}